  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe core idea is to use binary optimization with a periodic structure to minimize the maximum correlation. The key insight is that an optimal solution likely consists of a periodic pattern where half the period has ones and the other half has zeros, maximizing the minimum period separation. This approach differs from the parent solution's gradient-based optimization by directly: 1) Representing the function as a binary vector with fixed sum, 2) Using a genetic algorithm with crossover/mutation to preserve feasibility, and 3) Evaluating via full cross-correlation mode. This structure promotes large minimum separation between high/low regions, directly reducing maximum correlation.\n\nImportant limitations:\n- Repair relies on greedy bit flips for large deviations\n- Fitness evaluation is O(n\u00b2) due to correlation\n- No guarantee of global optimality\n- Solutions are confined to binary patterns\n\nMajor improvements:\n- Explicit periodicity enhances generalization\n- Binary representation enforces constraints directly\n- Repair preserves feasibility during mutation\n- Simpler transition between resolutions\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nRepresentation: heps. Each individual is a binary vector of length n_points, containing exactly n_points/2 ones (for n_points even)\nConstraint: \u2211h[i] = n_points/2 (dx=2/n_points ensures integral=1)\nObjectives:\nMaximize minimum separation principle. Compute correlation as:\n    corr_full = np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')\n    C5 = max(corr_full) * dx\nFittings:\n- Binary representation (exact 0/1)\n- Sum constraint strictly enforced\n- Forward-difference mutation handles periodicity\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nInitialize using parent if available:\n    initial_arr = initial_h_values if provided \n    h0 = repair_parent(initial_arr, n_points)\nGenerate population:\n    - Repair parent solution\n    - 49 random binary vectors with target sum\nDefine repair function to maintain binary:\n    - Round values then repair to target sum via bit flips\nEvalue fitness via correlation\n    - Compute cross-correlation with mode='full' via numpy\n    - Scale by dx to match objective\nGenetic operations:\n    - Two-point crossover (prob 0.7)\n    - Forward mutation (prob 0.3): flip bits and repair\n    - Maintain feasibility by immediate repair\nMain loop:\n    - Repeat 500 generations or time limit\n    - Update best solution periodically\n    - Repair to ensure population feasibility\nFinal output:\n    - Best solution h and C5\n    - Selected n_points (fixed 170-200)\n\n4. Search choices and variations\nInitialize with (n_points, target_sum):\n    Period length n_points (170-200 range)\n    target_sum = n_points/2\nFitness scaling:\n    Normalized by ideal minimal correlation\nMutation strategy:\n    Forward mutation preferred (preserves order)\nDrawback:\n    Limited hyperparameter exploration\n    Fitness scaling non-standard\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nRepresentational cost: O(n_points\u00b2) per fitness eval\nTime estimate: n_points=170 takes ~50ms evaluation\nMax generations: 500\nPotentially total wall-clock: 500*50ms ~ 40s\nScaling: For larger n_points, fewer generations needed (generation count inversely proportional to fitness improvement)\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nFunction: (h_values: np.ndarray, c5_bound: float, n_points: int)\nCheck:\n    All values 0/1 exactly\n    Sum equals target_sum within 1e-5\n    Correlation computation identical to evaluator\nBoundary cases handled by repair: \n    If mutation exceeds binary bounds, repair occurs\nFallback: Uses provided parent if cleared (otherwise strict repair)\nValidation:\n    For each candidate: verify binary and sum\n    Measure time taken (break if > budget_s)\nException handling:\n    Repair any NaN/inf values\n    Early stop if stuck (no improvement for 50 generations)\nReturn:\n    Best solution from all generations",
